<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latifundium</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as latifundia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A latifundium was a large agricultural estate in the Roman world, typically owned by a wealthy landowner and worked by enslaved labour or tenants. Such estates spread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the provinces as wealth became concentrated after Roman conquests. They are commonly associated with the decline of small farmers and with social tensions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latifundium.jpg" alt="Latifundium" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boyd Dwyer · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dominus_Julius_mosaic_in_the_Bardo_National_Museum(12240864473)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around three-quarters of Italians were illiterate in 1861, most spoke regional dialects rather than the Tuscan-based standard language, and the south suffered from <b>latifundia</b>, poverty and malaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Agriculture formed the economic backbone of the empire, organised around both the small family farm (fundus) and the large slave-worked estate (latifundium), and such <b>latifundia</b> came to dominate much of the Italian and North African countryside.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
